export const body = "<h2>Three valuable lessons from the project for ADs and TLs</h2>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span></p>\n\n<ul><li>Hello managers, we have some project management lessons from the project!</li><li>Lesson 1: Have a strike team to investigate technical requirements early on</li><li>Lesson 2: Tutorial videos</li><li>Lesson 3: Internal sharing and quizzing</li><li>In summary</li></ul>\n\n<h3>Hello managers, we have some project management lessons from the project!</h3>\n\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/1e676b8730e3231968d8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/1e676b8730e3231968d8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"608\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Without questions, managing this project was a daunting challenge. Last time, we learned how the two artists wrote <em>The Book of Solution </em>to alleviate the stress on the TAs in the project. But that story was just one piece of this management puzzle.</p>\n\n\n<h3>Lesson 1: Have a strike team to investigate technical requirements early on</h3>\n\n<p><strong>We all know the project was a huge success on the art side in the end, but what was it like when you picked it up?</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>This project was very different because it was the first time we worked a full-level project in VR. It was the first time we tried to achieve HD visuals and 90 FPS at the same time.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Interesting, VR quality must be challenging? Can you tell us more about the HD visuals and 90 FPS?</strong></p>\n\n<p>In VR, everything has to be extra detailed because players can view our assets up-close. We asked the client what the benchmark was, and the client suggested <em>Watch Dogs 2</em>'s level of quality. But we thought we could push it a bit further to exceed their expectations. However, this is how we got ourselves in trouble.</p>\n\n<p>When we started, we didn't understand how challenging the 90 FPS target was; later in production we realized, if we pushed the graphics, it would hurt the frame rate, and vice versa. Thus, making the game look great and run at 90 FPS was agonizing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Wow, that's unexpected, that must've been difficult.</strong></p>\n\n<p>We were transitioning from making VR assets to creating full VR levels. It's like building a car, you know. Our car must hit 90 km/h, and we knew how to make all the parts that go into it. But we didn't know how to put it all together. It took us a while to figure things out, but we managed to push through.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/cf3a6132c901e7be173d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/cf3a6132c901e7be173d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"288\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>What did you do to resolve that?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Of course, we didn't want to make any compromise to the quality. I worked with the artist (Senior Art Director) and the artist (Technical Art Director) to initiate learning activities and collaborated with our TAs to find solutions. For example, the artist wrote some plugins to work with HISM (Hierarchical Instanced Static Mesh) to ease the memory load. It worked for a while until Unreal updated HISM, which forced us to rework the levels to match the new functions. Basically, I and my core team members (TLs, acting leads) were working closely with TAs to iron out the issues all the time.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/b9880df5c3eb1fdcdc68.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/b9880df5c3eb1fdcdc68.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"319\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>VR learning in the project</p>\n\n<p>Useful VR tutorials they used:</p>\n\n<ul><li>\"Getting Started in VR with Unreal Engine\" course</li><li>Searched for VR content on 80 Level</li></ul>\n\n<p><strong>Can you give us an example?</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>I can give you an example. We had a level on a moving train. The player goes in and out of it to engage in different combat scenarios. So we suggested keeping the train static and moving the scene around. But the client insisted on moving the train, so we stuck with that.</p>\n\n<p>Three months into production, we realized it was impossible to move the train, so the client agreed with our \"static train\" approach. We spent two months on it, but it was also not good enough. After much trial-and-error, we ended up dividing the level into parts and made the environment move along with the camera. That solution saved a lot of memory usage for the system.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/79d5494468557122bf3c.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/79d5494468557122bf3c.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"323\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>Is there something you want to tell other teams about this lesson?</strong></p>\n\n<p>It was a painful process to learn that we should've had a dedicated team to look into the technical requirements in advance. If we had studied the technical aspects early on, we would've had an easier time with optimization. Like with the train example, if we knew what was feasible and stuck to it, we wouldn't have to waste a lot of time on iteration.</p>\n\n<p>The key takeaway is: deploy a strike team to investigate the technical requirements of your project as early as possible. It would be ideal to have 3 months' lead time before we commence big E2E projects:</p>\n\n<ul><li>AD + TL + TA to form a strike team to research the project's requirements</li><li>Strike team to propose / communicate with the client about the most efficient production pipeline and come to an agreement</li><li>Strike team to study the limitations of the engine, and understand the technical specs clearly</li></ul>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>How much time do you often have to study the technical requirements before full production?</p>\n\n<p><em>Example</em><em>: I had 2 weeks, but it wasn't enough. I wish I had 3 full weeks to focus on studying the requirements.</em></p>\n\n<p>Type Here</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson 2: Tutorial videos</h3>\n\n<p><strong>You have a huge team, 120 artists, so we are very curious about how you managed to train them.</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>In the beginning, our TLs gathered their team members and held live training sessions. But as the team expanded (internally and externally with off-site partners), it became increasingly difficult to repeat those sessions. So, we decided to make tutorial videos instead. That way, we can skip the live training and newcomers still have guidance when they join the project.</p>\n\n<p><strong>What were these tutorials like?</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>We approached them like hands-on training. We try to show the whole workflows in our videos and then add subtitles, so artists can play them on one screen and work on their assets on the other. This allowed them to learn and work simultaneously.</p>\n\n<p>And it's fine if the video quality is rough. We didn't have enough time to make them look perfect and polished. As long as a video illustrates the workflow clearly for artists to follow, we are cool with it. Because if the artists didn't follow them, we would be in big trouble. Real big trouble.</p>\n\n<p><strong>How do you make these tutorials?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Step 1: Do your own creation, tape it all.</p>\n\n<p>Step 2: Cut useless operations (the idle time when you stop to think, or some mistakes you make)</p>\n\n<p>Step 3: If we have time we would add audio, or add simple subtitles; if not, silent videos worked for this project anyway.</p>\n\n<p>It only took our core team members about a day per video.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/16e8d2f767ccfd4b1ce0.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/16e8d2f767ccfd4b1ce0.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>So who were making these tutorials?</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>Technically everyone could make one. We didn't assign tutorial making to people based on their seniority. Instead, the AD chose who was best for the job. So, an asset tutorial would be done by a great asset maker. Same for level dressing and baking. The artist (the co-author of <em>The Book of Solutions</em>) made a lot of useful tutorials actually.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Can we have a look at one of these tutorials?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Of course. This is an introduction for asset market map.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The result</strong></p>\n\n<p>Those videos saved the TLs plenty of time on coaching to focus on quality control.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Have your team ever created project-specific tutorials?</p>\n\n<p>Type Here</p>\n\n<h3>Lesson 3: Internal sharing and quizzing</h3>\n\n<p><strong>Earlier you mentioned organizing learning material, so we would like to know more about that.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Since we had plenty of things to learn, we wanted to ensure knowledge is <em>recorded</em>, <em>shared</em>, and <em>absorbed</em>. To do that, we promoted internal sharing on our Wiki page, and we quizzed our team members to make sure they were learning properly.</p>\n\n<p><strong>How did your team handle this sharing process?</strong></p>\n\n<p>Our ADs and TAs documented important messages and the client's feedback on our Wiki. Once the Wiki had been updated, our key members would relay new information to other members.</p>\n\n<p><sub>Side note</sub><sub>: key members in the game were Level Leads who broke down estimates, assigned tasks, and checked quality and deliveries.</sub></p>\n\n<p><strong>Tell us more about the quizzes!</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>The quizzes occurred spontaneously during our bi-weekly meetings. We often asked about minor details scattered in our Wiki page, so our team members couldn't answer unless they already read the page thoroughly. For example, I knew it was very important for my team members to know the right way to edit P4 descriptions, so I once displayed 10 P4 descriptions on the screen and asked them to spot the errors.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/library-inline/a63b669abc4f0d263338.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/library-inline/a63b669abc4f0d263338.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"692\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><strong>We see. Only the ADs could quiz people? And when do you guys do it?</strong><strong><br></strong></p>\n\n<p>Not just ADs but also TLs. And sometimes we do it the other way around: letting the junior guys quiz the senior guys instead.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The results</strong></p>\n\n<p>They avoided repeating mistakes that the client pointed out across all the levels they were making.</p>\n\n<p>The team developed a sense of quality control, eliminating minor issues and errors.</p>\n\n<p>The TLs understood how to read and analyze feedback to make their levels better.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Have you ever quizzed your team like this?</p>\n\n<p>Type Here</p>\n\n<h3>In summary</h3>\n\n<p>Despite the many challenges, the team was still able to deliver the game they set out to make. These are 3 valuable lessons they have to share:</p>\n\n<ul><li><strong>Have a dedicated strike team to investigate the technical requirements early</strong><strong><br></strong></li><li><strong>Make tutorial videos that artists can follow when working</strong></li><li><strong>Internal sharing &amp; quizzing to align all team members</strong><strong><br></strong></li></ul>\n\n<p>The artist believes these lessons are very valuable for those who are working, or about to work, on end-to-end projects.</p>\n\n<p>Before we say goodbye, we have one final question.</p>\n\n<p><span class=\"source-divider\" role=\"separator\"></span>Which lesson are you going to apply to your next project?</p>\n\n<p>ThemesBuild a technical strike teamCreate tutorial videosInternal sharing &amp; quizzingAll of them!</p>\n\n<p>Thank you for reading and happy creating!</p>";
export const images = ["assets/library-inline/16e8d2f767ccfd4b1ce0.webp", "assets/library-inline/1e676b8730e3231968d8.webp", "assets/library-inline/79d5494468557122bf3c.webp", "assets/library-inline/a63b669abc4f0d263338.webp", "assets/library-inline/b9880df5c3eb1fdcdc68.webp", "assets/library-inline/cf3a6132c901e7be173d.webp"];
